  "prompt_text": "The figure compares four network or communication architectures, moving from classical graph layouts to a quantum transport schematic. On the left, a centralized network is shown as a hub-and-spoke structure, with one large central node connected directly to many smaller peripheral nodes. Next, a decentralized network breaks this single hub into several local hubs, each serving a small cluster and linked by longer connections. The distributed network then becomes a mesh-like graph in which nodes are more evenly connected and there is no obvious controlling center.\n\nThe right side presents a quantum version. A lattice of nodes sits above a device-like channel, with highlighted wave-like shapes suggesting localized quantum states or excitations on parts of the network. Below, two end regions labeled A and B are connected through a central section labeled S. Arrows point in opposite directions from this middle region, indicating transfer or propagation between the two sides. The repeated wave packets in the channel seem to illustrate quantum transport through the structure rather than simple point-to-point wiring. Overall, the figure contrasts increasingly noncentralized classical connectivity with a quantum network where communication appears to rely on delocalized states and transmission through an intermediate system.",
